From b5ab49d7a50482e6e47e76a6720467211c9019c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Andrei V. Lepikhov" <lepihov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 10:19:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH v0 07/10] Use an int128 fast sum in sum(numeric) and
 avg(numeric)

The transition state keeps its running sum in NumericSumAccum: two separately
palloc'd int32 digit arrays, costing three allocations per group per aggregate.
The integer aggregates have long used a flat int128 state instead
(959277a4f57), but could do so only because their sums provably fit: numeric
has no such static bound -- a numeric(32,2) column overflows int128 after 1.7
million rows in one group.

Teach NumericAggState to keep the running sum in an int128 coefficient with
a fixed decimal scale for as long as it provably fits, and to promote -
exactly, and only once - into the digit-array accumulator on the first input
or intermediate that does not.  Aggregate results are bit-identical to
the previous coding in all cases: display scale follows the widest input dscale
and NaN/Infinity handling is untouched.

Combining, serialisation, and the moving-aggregate inverse transition keep
the int128 representation wherever it fits. The serialised format is unchanged.
Variance-family aggregates never use it. The per-row conversion into the int128
coefficient is not free. Values with up to 16 significant digits use an int64
path and cost about as much as accum_sum_add(); wider values use the int128
path, which is slower per row than digit-array addition. The gain therefore
comes from per-group work: allocations, carry propagation and finalisation.
A sum of wide values over a few large groups may regress slightly.
